-# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 # Design analysis for the D3 variant
-#
 # a) Concrete behavior:
-#    Each grid contains exactly two edge palettes on opposite sides: either
-#    left+right or top+bottom. The two palettes may share border-key colors,
-#    but shared keys map to different fill colors. Example:
-#      left palette contains 4->2, right palette contains 4->7.
-#      A hollow border-4 sprite near the left side fills with 2, while a
-#      hollow border-4 sprite near the right side fills with 7.
-#    Original logic would collapse everything into one mapping and cannot
-#    disambiguate shared keys; the variant therefore changes the palette-read
-#    stage and the sprite-to-palette assignment stage.
-#
+#    The input now contains two separate palette blocks on two different edges.
+#    One palette is horizontal on the top or bottom edge; the other is vertical on
+#    the left or right edge. Each palette still uses the old edge-dependent
+#    reading direction locally, but the solver must detect and read both in the
+#    same grid. Example trace:
+#      - Original task: one top palette [border row / fill row] explains every
+#        fillable sprite in the grid.
+#      - Variant: a top palette may map 2->7 and 4->6, while a left palette maps
+#        8->3 and 5->1. A hollow sprite bordered by color 4 must use the top
+#        palette; another bordered by color 8 must use the left palette.
+#      - Difference: the old "find one best palette and apply it globally"
+#        behavior leaves one whole sprite group unresolved.
 # b) Complexity calibration:
-#    The design uses only two palettes and one spatial grouping axis per grid,
-#    so the rule is learnable from 2-4 examples. To make the change visible,
-#    every generated puzzle uses at least one shared border color that is
-#    actually used by sprites on both sides and mapped differently.
-#
-# c) Implicit-assumption audit against the old main():
-#    - _best_palette assumed one globally best palette. This fails here because
-#      both palettes are valid and necessary.
-#    - _palette_mapping assumed border color uniquely determines fill globally.
-#      That is false once the same border color appears in both palettes.
-#    - _fill_enclosed_bg_regions picked only one global mapping, which becomes
-#      incorrect for shared keys. The new solver must choose a palette per hole.
-#
+#    Exactly two palettes are generated, with disjoint border-key colors. Each
+#    palette has 2-3 entries and contributes at least one actually used sprite,
+#    so both legends matter visually. One distractor sprite whose border color is
+#    in neither palette is retained from the original task.
+# c) Implicit-assumption review:
+#    The original _best_palette/_palette_mapping pipeline assumes there is a
+#    single maximal candidate. That is no longer safe: selecting only one palette
+#    would silently drop valid border->fill pairs for the second sprite group.
+#    The new solver therefore enumerates all edge runs that look like palettes,
+#    expects exactly two isolated legends, reads them separately, and only then
+#    merges their mappings.
